# The id classes the client and the server both hold, and every place the two
# tables disagree, with the reason each disagreement is allowed to stand.
#
# The client numbers species, moves, items, maps, flags and vars the way the
# engine's generated/*.txt does, those constants are its enums. The server
# numbers them the way codegen rendered its registries. Both halves round-trip
# their own table, so until tools/table_diff.py walked them side by side, a class
# the two disagreed about was invisible from inside either one. That is how the
# item map spent four phases translating ids that were already the same number.
#
# Each row is <class> <kind> <ids> <reason>. The kinds:
#
#   missing  an id the engine has and the server's table does not
#   extra    an id the server has and the engine's table does not
#   named    an id both hold, under names that are not the same name
#   absent   the class has no server-side table at all (ids: all)
#
# The comparison is exact in both directions. An undeclared disagreement fails,
# and so does a row here that is no longer true, a gap that closes has to be
# written down as closed. tools/table_diff.py runs in `make test` as
# tests/tablediff_test.sh, and standalone as `make tablediff`.

# The twelve mail slots the two worlds fill with different mail, and the item
# Gen 5 renamed. Same id, same slot, different word: the shared item table
# carries them deliberately and idmap_gen_test.sh names them again from the
# other side. Renaming one of them here would be a silent content change.
items  named  58        the engine's X_DEFENSE is the server's X Defend, one item under two generations' names
items  named  137-148   the twelve mail slots the two worlds fill with different mail (6.8.4)

# Sinnoh's flags and vars now have a server side: the story generator reads the
# DS decomp's own list as well, so SinnohFlags and SinnohVars carry 4,390 of the
# 4,402 entries the engine's list names. The values are a running count and not
# line numbers, an entry written `NAME = OTHER` re-anchors it, and both
# halves read it that way.
#
# What is left is names rather than ids. Nine of the twelve are block boundaries
# (`*_START`, `*_END`, `SYSTEM_FLAGS_FIRST_ARRIVAL_TO_ZONE`), each an alias for
# the entry beside it, so the id is held on the server under that entry's real
# name and only the marker's own spelling is absent. The other three are the
# `FR_VAR_*` script-local slots, which carry no `VAR_` name for the generator to
# emit.
flags  missing 1,730,1360,2480,2720,16384,16416,16672,32768   a block boundary alias: the server holds the id under the real entry that shares it
flags  missing 32782-32784                                    FR_VAR_0x800E..0x8010, script-local slots with no VAR_ name

# The 156 species and 92 moves Black adds, which reached the server out of a
# cartridge and the client not at all. The server's tables are the game's rules
# and can hold a species the engine has never heard of; the client's are what it
# can DRAW, and pl_pokegra stops at Arceus because the game it was built for did
# (mmo/include/sprite.h). So this is not a gap to close by editing a table: the
# art is a cartridge import. Black's a/0/0/4 is a Gen 5 archive of 14285
# compressed members in a layout the engine's loader does not read, so it is
# assembled into this game's own sheets on the way in.
#
# The client stopped refusing these ids on 2026-08-30. Seven archives it indexes
# by species, the personal data, the learnset, the evolutions, the icon, the
# name, the battle sheets and the byte that seats them, are filled out of the
# same cartridge by tools/portspecies.py (the sheets through
# tools/portsprites.py), so a Gen 5 species has stats, a type, a name, a face
# in the box grid and its own picture in a battle. So this row is about a FILL,
# not about ids: both halves NAME all 649, and the client draws them once a
# cartridge is imported.
#
# The moves crossed the same evening, through tools/portmoves.py: the 16-byte
# table, three text banks, the animation scripts and the per-move battle stubs,
# 468..559, out of the same cartridge, with the 467 shared moves as the oracle
# (they rebuild this game's own table bar the 108 changes Gen 5 made). What a
# ported move has NOT got on the client is its Gen 5 effect: the engine's battle
# scripts stop at 276, so the 64 moves whose effect Gen 5 invented are plain HIT
# or Splash's nothing there. The SERVER decides what a move does, and its turn
# engine is a Gen 3 core (damage, stat stages, accuracy), so the 61 effects fall
# in three piles (server.game/.../battle/EffectTable.kt): 24 are real, the
# multi-stat dances and guards (Hone Claws, Quiver Dance, Shell Smash, Coil,
# Shift Gear, Work Up, Cotton Guard, Autotomize), the riders (Flame Charge, Acid
# Spray, Glaciate, V-create), Clear Smog, the field-dependent powers (Stored
# Power, Electro Ball, Acrobatics), Chip Away, Foul Play, Psyshock's kind, Storm
# Throw's crit, Final Gambit, Synchronoise, Guard Split and Power Split; about
# twenty are damaging moves whose rider needs a status, the weather, an item, a
# weight or a partner the server has no notion of yet, and land as base damage
# like their Gen 4 cousins (Scald, Hurricane, Heavy Slam, the Pledges); and the
# rest are status moves that cannot act here and fail visibly (the rooms,
# Telekinesis, Soak, Heal Pulse, the guards, After You, Quash). No Gen 5 move
# has an animation of its own: each borrows a Gen 4 move's, named row by row in
# mmo/MOVE_ANIMS.
# THE EVOLUTIONS AND THE ABILITY NAMES CROSSED ON 2026-09-06, and both were
# holes rather than decisions.
#
# evo.narc was filled with 44 zero bytes a species on the stated grounds that
# "the server owns whether a monster evolves". It does not. Nothing on the
# server ever sends the evolution prompt, EvolutionPromptPacket has a codec
# and no sender, because the CLIENT runs the game's own evolution screen off
# that archive and the server only holds the report against its table
# afterwards (EvolutionRegistry.isReachable). So a Gen 5 monster levelled up
# forever and never evolved, with a server standing ready to accept a report
# that could not arrive. tools/portspecies.py carries the cartridge's own rows
# now: 72 of the 156 have somewhere to go, and the oracle is the archive itself,
# 492 of the 493 shared species rebuild this game's own member byte for byte
# out of Black, the one exception being Feebas, which Gen 5 gave the Prism Scale
# trade beside its beauty evolution. Two rows are dropped by name: Karrablast
# and Shelmet trade for each other, which is Gen 5's own method and has no Gen 4
# number and no mechanic here. The evolution TARGET is written as the ENGINE's
# species id, so it goes through the Victini/Snivy translation in
# mmo/include/species_port.h; nothing in Gen 5 evolves into either, and the
# translation is there so it stays right.
#
# The ability NAME banks (610, 611, 612) stopped at Bad Dreams, 123, while the
# personal entries tools/portspecies.py synthesises had been carrying Gen 5
# ability ids since the species port. So every ported species named an ability
# nothing could draw, and the client's own display table said so by name
# ("ability: id has no name in the engine's 124 or the official client overlay").
# tools/portabilities.py fills all three from the cartridge, 124..164. Its
# oracle is that all 123 ids both games name are spelled IDENTICALLY in Black's
# bank and in this game's, a shift of one slot would break all 123 at once,
# plus the measured rule that the upper case bank is the title one upper-cased,
# which holds over this game's 124 and Black's 165. mmo/src/display_data.gen.h
# is grown to 165 from the same text, committed under codegen/gen5 so that
# generator still runs on the engine tree alone.
#
# AND THE CLIENT'S OWN BATTLE ANSWERS FOR TEN OF THEM, since later the same day.
# The engine's generated/abilities.h defines exactly 124 constants, so its battle
# code had no case for anything past Bad Dreams and no name to write one with,
# which mattered more than the server's half, because a wild or trainer fight is
# computed HERE and only reported. mods/openmmo/src/openmmo_ability.c is the
# answer and mods/openmmo/include/openmmo_ability_ids.h is where the ids come
# from, generated out of the same codegen/gen5/enums.json the server's enum and
# the display table are built from, so an id means one thing in all three.
#
# Every one of the ten sits at a site the engine already asked a question at, so
# each patch is a clause added to a condition rather than a new mechanism:
# Turboblaze and Teravolt in Battler_IgnorableAbility beside Mold Breaker,
# Prankster on the priority the turn order compares, Sand Rush beside Swift Swim
# and Chlorophyll, Defeatist on the attack stat beside Hustle and Guts, Sheer
# Force and Sand Force on the move's power in the same block (and Sheer Force
# again at BtlCmd_CheckEffectActivation, because a move paid 30% for its rider
# does not also get the rider), Victory Star and Wonder Skin on the hit rate
# beside Compound Eyes, Iron Barbs sharing Rough Skin's whole case through a
# one-line rename that lasts for that switch alone, and Big Pecks beside Keen Eye
# and Hyper Cutter, whose message reads the ability off the monster, so it
# names itself now that the bank is filled.
#
# THE OTHER SIXTEEN ARE NOT WRITTEN, and the reasons differ. Moxie, Defiant,
# Justified and Weak Armor each raise or lower a stat with a message of their
# own, which is a battle SUBSCRIPT and therefore script data in battle/skill/
# rather than code; Cursed Body, Mummy and Poison Touch need an effect the engine
# has no subscript for at all. Regenerator, Unnerve, Infiltrator and Overcoat
# each want a site openmmo_ability.c does not reach yet (a switch-out hook, the
# berry check, the screen checks, the weather damage step) and are ordinary work.
# Illusion needs the display layer rather than the battle one. Healer and
# Telepathy act only on an ALLY and this client runs no double battles, so inert
# is the correct answer for those two and not a gap.
#
# The SERVER decides what an ability does in the fights IT runs (PvP, duels,
# static battles), and since 2026-09-06 it decides something too. The two lists
# do not match and should not: the client's battle has weather, status, items and
# healing, and the server's Gen 3 core has none of them.
# server.game/.../battle/AbilityTable.kt is the same three-pile split
# EffectTable.kt keeps. Real: the mould breakers, the type immunities and Wonder
# Guard, the attack and power multipliers (Huge Power, Pure Power, Hustle,
# Defeatist, the four pinch abilities, Technician, Sheer Force, Reckless,
# Analytic, Rivalry), Adaptability, Tinted Lens, Filter, Solid Rock, Multiscale,
# Thick Fat, Heatproof, Dry Skin, the crit abilities (Battle Armor, Shell Armor,
# Super Luck, Sniper, Anger Point), the accuracy ones (Compound Eyes, No Guard,
# Victory Star, Wonder Skin), the stage ones (Clear Body, White Smoke, Hyper
# Cutter, Keen Eye, Big Pecks, Contrary, Simple, Unaware, Defiant), Moxie,
# Justified, Rattled, Weak Armor, Rough Skin, Iron Barbs, Sturdy, Damp,
# Prankster, Stall, Serene Grace and Shield Dust. Inert, each waiting on a
# system the turn engine has not got: everything needing weather, a status
# condition, a held item, healing, a switch-in hook, a turn counter or an ally.
# AbilityTable.INERT is that list and it is meant to shorten.
#
# Two things a battle got right for the first time with it: a monster now fights
# with ITS OWN ability rather than its species' first (the personality value
# picks the second slot, the Dream World bit the third), and BattleMonBlock's
# abilityId on the wire is that same answer, so a battle and the import legality
# check no longer disagree about what a monster has.
#
# THE MACHINES CROSSED THE SAME DAY, on both halves at once.
#
# Where Gen 5 keeps the table was the blocker and it is not on the items: Hone
# Claws, 468, appears nowhere as a u16 in Black's whole item archive. It is one
# table of 101 move ids in the ARM9, at 0x09AAA0, and it is the ONLY run of 101
# consecutive distinct valid move ids in the 682 KB image, which is how
# gen5_tables.machine_moves finds it rather than trusting an offset. Its layout
# explains itself: TM01..TM92, then HM01..HM06, then TM93..TM95, this game's
# own item-id block with two HMs dropped and three TMs put in the gap.
#
# THE PERSONAL ENTRY'S BIT ORDER IS A DIFFERENT ORDER, and reading the table's
# order into it is the mistake this nearly made: bits 0..94 are TM01..TM95 and
# bits 95..100 are HM01..HM06, so a machine's bit is its NUMBER. The bit columns
# said so, not an assumption, with the table's order the six HMs agreed with
# this game's own columns on 43% to 75% of the shared species against 96% for
# the TMs, and with the right order the same 75 columns come out at 99.79% mean,
# 59 of them perfect and none below 95%. What is left disagreeing is Gen 5
# changing its mind (Light Screen 95.9%, Safeguard 96.6%, Reflect 98.0%), and
# portspecies.check_machines holds the floors that measurement justifies.
#
# Machines are paired by the MOVE they teach and never by number, because the
# numbering is the one thing that does not cross: Gen 5's TM01 is Hone Claws
# where this game's is Focus Punch. Both halves do that pairing from the same two
# facts, tools/gen5_tables.py commits the move ids a ported species can be
# taught, and tools/portspecies.py writes this game's bitfield, and the two
# were checked against each other over all 156 species with zero disagreements.
#
# The server's half mattered more than it looks. GeneratedMoveSources held no row
# at all for 494-649, and MoveSourceRegistry answering null is "nothing here
# knows", but the level up learnsets for those species have been there since
# the species port, so LocalScriptService already had a table to hold them to and
# refused every move that was not on it. A ported species could not legally be
# taught a machine move, and the report saying it had been was written down as a
# cheat. 155 of the 156 have a machine list now.
#
# WHAT IS NOT CARRIED, and is not a gap: 25 of this game's 100 machines teach a
# move Gen 5 has no machine for (Focus Punch, Water Pulse, Bullet Seed, Giga
# Drain, Iron Tail, Roost, Dragon Pulse, Stealth Rock, Dark Pulse and 16 more).
# Those bits are zero. Whether Excadrill could be taught a Stealth Rock TM is not
# a fact sitting in an image nobody read, Gen 5 has no such machine and Gen 4
# has no such species, so no cartridge this project reads states it. Writing zero
# is the same refusal-rather-than-guess the rest of these tables make, and the
# question is closed rather than open. Egg moves and tutors are a different
# absence: Black has no tutors at all, which is the true answer, and its egg move
# archive has not been read, which is the same silence as before rather than a
# claim that a ported species hatches knowing nothing.
species extra 494-649   Black's, from mmo/tools/gen5_tables.py; the client names and draws them once a cartridge is imported, battle sprite included, and since 2026-08-30 every species' battle sprite is Black's looping one, baked to eight frames a face (mmo/ASSETS.md)
moves    extra  468-559   Black's, from mmo/tools/gen5_tables.py; the client names and serves them once a cartridge is imported (tools/portmoves.py): table, name, description, a borrowed animation (mmo/MOVE_ANIMS), and HIT or Splash's nothing for an effect the engine does not have
